WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.506
SARA ROBINSON: The
best way to see

00:00:01.506 --> 00:00:03.480
how AutoML Vision and
natural language work

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.940
is through live demos.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.630
Let's start with vision.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.580
To show you AutoML
Vision, we'll look

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.680
at a model I've built for
predicting the species

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.960
of a leaf given an image.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.900
The data for this is sourced
from the Leafsnap dataset,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.600
and our model was trained on
images of 10 different types

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.490
of leaves.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.300
Here, in the AutoML Vision UI,
is where I gather and label

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.270
all of the images that will
be used to train my model.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.540
I can upload them
directly to the UI,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.510
and I only need 10 images
per label to start training.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.730
The best part about
AutoML is that I

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.700
don't need to write any of
the model code to train this.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.390
I can simply press the Train
button to train my model.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:47.220
This particular model took
exactly one hour to train.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.830
When training completes, I
can take a look at metrics

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.370
to evaluate accuracy.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.655
This is what's called
the confusion matrix.

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.790
It tells us the percentage
of images for each label

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.520
that our model is able
to classify correctly.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.239
Ideally, we'll see a strong
diagonal down the top left,

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:05.780
since this means
our model classified

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.100
a high percentage of images
in our test set correctly.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.140
And we can click on any cell
to see specific images where

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.600
our model got confused.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.930
Finally, the most important
part of this process

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.700
is generating
predictions on images

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.220
our model hasn't seen before.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.590
Let's try our model out
by generating predictions

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.320
on two images of leaves.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.300
As someone who's
not a plant expert,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.210
I'd probably classify
both of these as leaves,

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:36.195
or, if we're being very
technical, green leaves.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:37.445
Let's see what our model says.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.490
Turns out these both belong
to different species,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.830
and our model has predicted
both varieties correctly

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.930
with over 96% confidence.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.110
Once we have a
trained model, AutoML

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.430
gives us access to a
custom REST API endpoint

00:01:58.430 --> 00:01:59.900
to make predictions.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.900
If I add more images and train
a new version of my model,

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.680
I only need to change the
model ID in my API request.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.360
I am incredibly
excited about AutoML

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.430
because it gives
anyone the tools

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.040
to build custom models,
regardless of their machine

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.480
learning expertise.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.570
You might even say that
AutoML Vision is turning over

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.680
a new leaf in the way we build
image classification models.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.620
And although I've shown plant
species classification here,

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.500
you could easily build a
model in any other domain.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.990
We've seen our alpha users
build AutoML Vision models

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:32.780
for identifying damaged
products before they're shipped,

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.000
classifying
catalog-specific images,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.150
and identifying points in time,
like stages of construction.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.460
But what about other kinds
of information, like text

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.740
documents or news articles?

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.920
With AutoML natural
language, it's just as easy

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.650
to build a custom model to
make sense of written content.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.200
Take donorschoose.org,
for example,

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.650
a nonprofit that receives
hundreds of thousands

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.930
of proposals each year for
classroom projects in need

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.250
of funding.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.080
They currently rely on
a team of volunteers

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.910
to manually screen
each submission,

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.280
so they published a
dataset to Kaggle in hopes

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.170
that a community of
machine learning experts

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.580
could build them an
automated solution.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.660
Let's see if AutoML
natural language can help.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.230
Similar to AutoML Vision, here I
can look at all of the examples

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.220
in my dataset and their
corresponding label.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.840
The text inputs to our
model our needs statements

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.790
from the teachers
detailing the materials

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.860
they need for their classroom.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.410
Each need request has
an associated category

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.890
to help direct the teacher's
requests to a relevant donor.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.330
The labels used
to train the model

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.850
have been assigned
manually by volunteers.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.410
Once the model is
trained, it will

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.160
be able to predict the category
of new requests automatically.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.860
To see how our model
performs on new text that

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.110
hasn't been
categorized yet, we'll

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.230
take the following needs
statement as an example.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.180
My students need
aquarium lighting

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.690
for our environmental
clownfish aquaculture project.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.420
Donors Choose needs to assign
the category Lab Equipment

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.160
to this request,
which isn't completely

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.357
obvious from the text itself.

00:04:01.357 --> 00:04:02.940
Let's try it out on
our trained model.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.570
We can see that our model was
able to correctly classify

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:12.300
this as lab equipment
with 98% confidence.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.050
Like AutoML Vision, I now
have access to a custom model

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.390
that I can use to generate
predictions on new texts

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.730
with one API request.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.560
That's really all
there is to it.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.425
Without writing
any model code, you

00:04:24.425 --> 00:04:25.800
can build custom
machine learning

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.050
models that can accurately
recognize your own images,

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.930
understand the contents
of your documents,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.285
and even translate your
jargon authentically.

00:04:33.285 --> 00:04:35.040
They're ready to
use immediately,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.890
accessible through Google Cloud,
and they scale automatically

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.770
to meet your needs.

